WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.987
Letting the model reason: where chain of thought came from.

2
00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:10.627
In 2022, Wei and colleagues published “Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models.

3
00:00:10.907 --> 00:00:18.565
” The method was simple: include a few worked examples whose answers show intermediate reasoning steps, not just the final answer.

4
00:00:18.845 --> 00:00:28.211
On arithmetic, commonsense, and symbolic reasoning tasks, this improved accuracy substantially, and the gains appeared in sufficiently large models.

5
00:00:28.491 --> 00:00:42.123
Their headline result: a 540-billion-parameter model prompted with eight chain-of-thought examples reached state-of-the-art accuracy on GSM8K, a benchmark of grade-school math word problems.

6
00:00:42.403 --> 00:00:52.707
The practical version became a staple: ask the model to think step by step before answering, and to put its final answer inside <answer> tags so code can extract it.

7
00:00:52.987 --> 00:00:57.701
Anthropic’s tutorial teaches exactly this, under the name “precognition.

8
00:00:57.981 --> 00:01:11.635
” The mechanism makes sense given week 1: the model generates one token at a time, so writing out intermediate steps gives later tokens something to build on, rather than forcing the answer to come out in the first few tokens.

9
00:01:11.915 --> 00:01:13.173
That is the history.

10
00:01:13.453 --> 00:01:19.768
The present is different, because the major providers now train models to reason before answering on their own.
